% Digits, a period and a digit are reserved for a floating-point literal and
% rejected at the number's first digit, after any sign.  The whole form is
% consumed, so the period inside it never ends the statement and each
% statement reports once.  Without the reservation the second rule would be
% a rule and a query.  Too many digits for an integer get the same message.
p(1.5).
q(X) :- p(X), X = 1.2.
2.5 > 1?
p(-1.5).
r(X) :- p(X), X = 1.2 > 1?
big(99999999999999999999.5).
